{
  "id": "5074e1f5-988b-4a09-927d-9150e9a3ccb1",
  "productId": "openmrs",
  "title": "Sticky Note Frontend-to-Database Code Path",
  "updatedAt": 1793547913899,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "7xr2ICOzjpe5UwL4",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "When a user adds a sticky note in the patient chart, what code runs from the frontend request down to the database write? List the frontend function that sends the request, the server-side REST handler, the core service implementation, and the data-access method.",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "A sticky note in the O3 patient chart is not a special database table — it is stored as an **Obs** (an \"observation\", OpenMRS's generic row for any clinical value recorded about a patient). So writing a sticky note is really just creating an Obs with a value of type text and a configured concept (\"concept\" = the dictionary term for what a value means). The write travels from the React frontend, over `/ws/rest/v1/obs` in the `webservices.rest` module, into OpenMRS core's ObsService, and finally through Hibernate to the database.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## The chain, top to bottom",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "| Layer | What runs | Where it lives |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Frontend UI action | `handleSave` (on the modal's Save button) | [1] |\n| Frontend request function | `createStickyNote` → `POST /ws/rest/v1/obs` | [2] |\n| REST handler (resource) | `ObsResource1_8.save` → `Context.getObsService().saveObs(...)` | [3] |\n| Core service | `ObsServiceImpl.saveObs` → `saveNewOrVoidedObs` → `dao.saveObs(obs)` | [4] |\n| Data access | `HibernateObsDAO.saveObs` → `HibernateUtil.saveOrUpdate` | [5] |",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4,
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Step by step",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**1. The user clicks Save in the modal.** `StickyNoteModal` renders a `TextArea` and a primary Save button bound to `handleSave` [6]. `handleSave` trims the text, ignores empty or unchanged input, then branches: in edit mode it calls `updateStickyNote`, otherwise (the \"add a new note\" case) it calls `createStickyNote(patientUuid, trimmed, stickyNoteConceptUuid)` [7]. The concept UUID comes from the app's configuration (`stickyNoteConceptUuid`), read via `useConfig` [8]. On success it shows a snackbar, revalidates with `mutate()`, and closes; on failure it shows an error snackbar [9].",
          "citations": [
            6,
            7,
            8,
            9
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**2. The frontend function that sends the request is `createStickyNote`.** It uses the framework's `openmrsFetch` to `POST` to `${restBaseUrl}/obs` with a JSON body of `person` (the patient UUID), `concept` (the configured concept UUID), `value` (the note text) and `obsDatetime` (now, in ISO format) [2]. Because the payload has no `encounter`, the note is saved as a standalone Obs — the file's own comment notes the UI only writes standalone obs, and the read hook filters out obs that belong to an encounter or come from a form [10].",
          "citations": [
            2,
            10
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**3. Server side, the REST handler is the Obs resource.** The `/obs` route is served by the `ObsResource1_8` class (annotated `@Resource(name = ...\"/obs\")`) [11]. A POST is turned into an `Obs` object by the delegating CRUD framework, which then calls the resource's `save` method; OpenMRS ships progressively newer subclasses — `ObsResource1_9`, `ObsResource1_11`, `ObsResource2_1`, `ObsResource2_7` — but those only add fields (like `status`/`interpretation`), and `save` is inherited from the 1_8 base [12]. `ObsResource1_8.save` calls `Context.getObsService().saveObs(delegate, \"REST web service\")`, then re-reads the saved Obs, which is the handoff into core [3].",
          "citations": [
            11,
            12,
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**4. The core service implementation is `ObsServiceImpl.saveObs`.** It rejects a null Obs, requires a change message when updating an existing one, and checks privileges (`ADD_OBS` for new, `EDIT_OBS` for existing) [4] and [13]. A brand-new note has no `obsId`, so it takes the `saveNewOrVoidedObs` branch, which handles any complex concept and then calls `dao.saveObs(obs)`, returning the persisted object [14]. (If a note already existed and was edited, the path is different: `saveExistingObs` creates a new Obs row via `Obs.newInstance`, saves it, and voids the old row to preserve history [15].)",
          "citations": [
            4,
            13,
            14,
            15
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**5. The data-access method is `HibernateObsDAO.saveObs`.** It explicitly saves any new group members first, then persists the Obs with `HibernateUtil.saveOrUpdate(sessionFactory.getCurrentSession(), obs)` — this is the actual Hibernate write that inserts the row into the `obs` table [5].",
          "citations": [
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## The flow at a glance",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: \"Sticky note write path\"\n---\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant UI as StickyNoteModal\n    participant Res as createStickyNote\n    participant REST as ObsResource (webservices.rest)\n    participant Svc as ObsServiceImpl\n    participant DAO as HibernateObsDAO\n    participant DB as Obs table\n    UI->>Res: handleSave calls createStickyNote\n    Res->>REST: POST /ws/rest/v1/obs\n    REST->>Svc: Context.getObsService().saveObs\n    Svc->>DAO: dao.saveObs(obs)\n    DAO->>DB: saveOrUpdate\n    DB-->>Svc: void\n    Svc-->>REST: saved Obs\n    REST-->>UI: response, then mutate and close\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Repositories involved",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- `github.com/openmrs/openmrs-esm-patient-chart` — the O3 patient chart microfrontend; the sticky-note feature lives in `esm-patient-notes-app`.\n- `github.com/openmrs/openmrs-module-webservices.rest` — exposes the `/ws/rest/v1/obs` REST endpoint.\n- `github.com/openmrs/openmrs-core` — the ObsService business layer and the Hibernate DAO that owns the database write.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Notes on what I verified and what I did not",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- I traced `createStickyNote` (the new-note path) through to `saveObs`/`dao.saveObs`. The edit path goes through `updateStickyNote` (a `POST` to `/obs/{uuid}`) [16], which hits the same resource but the service's `saveExistingObs` branch instead.\n- The exact framework code that receives the POST and calls the resource's `save` (the base delegating CRUD/controller) is not shown in this answer; I confirmed the resource-level entry (`ObsResource1_8.save`) and that it is the \"/obs\" handler, but I did not read the dispatcher that invokes it.\n- The sticky note's concept (a text datatype) is assumed from the payload shape (`value` as a plain string); I read the resource's `value`/representation handling but did not open the concept configuration to confirm the datatype.",
          "citations": [
            16
          ]
        }
      ],
